WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.109
Organizations increasingly want to pool their data
to build better machine learning models,

00:00:06.189 --> 00:00:11.798
but privacy policies and intellectual property laws
often make raw data sharing impossible.

00:00:11.878 --> 00:00:17.613
This paper introduces a privacy-preserving
framework that uses autoencoders to turn sensitive

00:00:17.693 --> 00:00:20.926
tabular data into obfuscated latent-space embeddings.

00:00:21.006 --> 00:00:23.114
Instead of exchanging raw features,

00:00:23.194 --> 00:00:26.928
collaborating peers share only these encoded
representations,

00:00:27.008 --> 00:00:30.054
then join them to train a shared downstream model.

00:00:30.134 --> 00:00:34.806
Across three public benchmarks, the encoded data
preserves predictive power,

00:00:34.886 --> 00:00:41.120
with performance dropping by less than ten
percentage points while the original features stay hidden.

00:00:41.500 --> 00:00:46.939
A common real-world situation is two companies that
each hold a different set of variables

00:00:47.019 --> 00:00:48.778
about the same group of users.

00:00:48.858 --> 00:00:51.047
If they could combine their features,

00:00:51.127 --> 00:00:56.566
both could predict a shared target variable far
more accurately and make better decisions.

00:00:56.646 --> 00:00:58.160
But the data is sensitive,

00:00:58.240 --> 00:01:03.556
so privacy policies and intellectual property laws
forbid handing over the raw features,

00:01:03.636 --> 00:01:07.174
even when the communication channel between them is
secure.

00:01:07.254 --> 00:01:12.386
The collaboration is cancelled, and the potential
boost in model performance is lost.

00:01:12.466 --> 00:01:17.598
The core problem this paper tackles is how to let
such peers share their information,

00:01:17.678 --> 00:01:20.971
and keep the predictive power of the original features,

00:01:21.051 --> 00:01:23.730
without ever exposing the raw sensitive data.

00:01:24.110 --> 00:01:28.609
Academia and industry have built several
privacy-preserving strategies,

00:01:28.689 --> 00:01:29.899
but each has limits.

00:01:29.980 --> 00:01:35.511
Encryption approaches like homomorphic encryption
offer strong security yet are hard to

00:01:35.591 --> 00:01:39.704
deploy in real settings because of their technology
requirements.

00:01:39.784 --> 00:01:43.380
Federated learning decentralizes training across devices,

00:01:43.460 --> 00:01:47.315
but it assumes every peer holds the same kind of
information,

00:01:47.395 --> 00:01:51.507
so it cannot handle two peers that contribute
different features.

00:01:51.587 --> 00:01:55.442
Differential privacy masks individual values by
adding noise,

00:01:55.522 --> 00:01:58.279
which can significantly reduce data utility.

00:01:58.359 --> 00:02:03.956
Linear dimensionality reduction such as Principal
Component Analysis obfuscates features

00:02:04.035 --> 00:02:06.987
but may lose important nonlinear relationships.

00:02:07.067 --> 00:02:12.663
These gaps motivate a new approach that uses deep
representation learning to encode data

00:02:12.743 --> 00:02:15.630
while keeping its predictive structure intact.

00:02:16.010 --> 00:02:19.310
The paper makes three main contributions.

00:02:19.390 --> 00:02:22.690
First,

00:02:22.770 --> 00:02:26.070
it reviews existing privacy-preserving machine
learning approaches to expose their limitations

00:02:26.150 --> 00:02:29.450
and the room for improvement.

00:02:29.530 --> 00:02:32.830
Second, it proposes a concrete framework in which
each peer trains an autoencoder,

00:02:32.910 --> 00:02:36.210
shares only the latent-space representation of its data,

00:02:36.290 --> 00:02:39.590
and then joins these embeddings to train a shared
supervised model.

00:02:39.670 --> 00:02:42.970
Third,

00:02:43.050 --> 00:02:46.350
it validates the framework on three public datasets
spanning regression and classification,

00:02:46.430 --> 00:02:49.730
and across five experimental scenarios,

00:02:49.810 --> 00:02:53.110
ranging from a raw-data baseline to non-naive
multitask autoencoders that also predict

00:02:53.190 --> 00:02:56.490
the target variable during encoding.

00:02:56.870 --> 00:03:01.780
The framework inserts one extra step into the usual
data-sharing pipeline.

00:03:01.860 --> 00:03:03.939
Instead of merging raw datasets,

00:03:04.019 --> 00:03:10.615
each peer first passes its features through an
autoencoder to obtain a latent-space representation,

00:03:10.695 --> 00:03:14.189
an obfuscated version of the data ready to be shared.

00:03:14.269 --> 00:03:20.259
The peers then join these representations using a
shared observation ID and train a single

00:03:20.339 --> 00:03:25.047
supervised model on the combined embeddings to
predict the same target.

00:03:25.127 --> 00:03:30.779
The autoencoder is fixed across all experiments so
conclusions reflect the framework,

00:03:30.859 --> 00:03:37.591
not network tuning: the encoder has four layers
going from N input features through hidden sizes 128,

00:03:37.671 --> 00:03:43.188
64, and 40 down to an embedding of size M, and the
decoder mirrors it back up to N.

00:03:43.268 --> 00:03:46.897
Every layer uses ReLU, the loss is Mean Absolute Error,

00:03:46.977 --> 00:03:51.483
and training uses the Adam optimizer with a
learning rate of 0.0001.

00:03:51.563 --> 00:03:53.304
In the non-naive scenarios,

00:03:53.384 --> 00:03:59.104
the autoencoder becomes a multitask network that
also predicts the objective variable,

00:03:59.184 --> 00:04:03.150
so the encoding is guided by the downstream
prediction task.

00:04:03.530 --> 00:04:07.538
To test the framework under realistic and varied
conditions,

00:04:07.618 --> 00:04:10.263
the authors chose three public datasets.

00:04:10.343 --> 00:04:15.509
House Pricing has about twenty-one thousand
observations and twelve features,

00:04:15.589 --> 00:04:19.734
with a regression target predicting house price in
US dollars.

00:04:19.814 --> 00:04:25.934
MNIST Numbers has thirty-five thousand observations
and seven hundred eighty-four features,

00:04:26.014 --> 00:04:30.430
treated as tabular data for a ten-class digit
classification task.

00:04:30.510 --> 00:04:36.699
Buzz in Social Media has over eighty-seven thousand
observations and seventy-seven features,

00:04:36.779 --> 00:04:38.334
again a regression task.

00:04:38.414 --> 00:04:43.784
Together they cover regression and classification,
small and large feature sets,

00:04:43.864 --> 00:04:45.692
and different feature types,

00:04:45.772 --> 00:04:50.802
letting the authors probe how robust and scalable
the encoding approach is.

00:04:50.882 --> 00:04:56.389
All experiments ran on a modest setup: a two-core
Intel Xeon CPU at 2.3 gigahertz,

00:04:56.469 --> 00:05:02.180
an Nvidia K80 or T4 GPU with twelve gigabytes of
memory, and twelve gigabytes of RAM.

00:05:02.560 --> 00:05:06.277
The central finding is that sharing encoded
representations instead of raw features barely

00:05:06.357 --> 00:05:10.074
hurts predictive performance.

00:05:10.154 --> 00:05:13.871
On House Pricing,

00:05:13.951 --> 00:05:17.669
the raw-data baseline reaches a test R-squared of
about ninety percent,

00:05:17.749 --> 00:05:21.466
and the two-peer scenario using individual
autoencoders still reaches about eighty-nine percent,

00:05:21.546 --> 00:05:25.263
an almost negligible drop despite the data being
obfuscated.

00:05:25.343 --> 00:05:29.060
On Buzz in Social Media,

00:05:29.140 --> 00:05:32.857
the baseline R-squared is around ninety-six percent
and the encoded scenarios stay in

00:05:32.937 --> 00:05:36.654
the high eighties to low nineties.

00:05:36.734 --> 00:05:40.451
For MNIST digit classification,

00:05:40.531 --> 00:05:44.249
test accuracy moves from ninety-two percent at
baseline down to the mid-eighties under encoding.

00:05:44.329 --> 00:05:48.046
Importantly, the non-naive multitask scenarios,

00:05:48.126 --> 00:05:51.843
where the autoencoder is guided by the target
variable, recover much of this gap,

00:05:51.923 --> 00:05:55.640
showing that shaping the latent space with the
downstream task improves results.

00:05:56.020 --> 00:06:02.490
The experiments are organized as an ablation across
five scenarios that isolate each design decision.

00:06:02.570 --> 00:06:06.771
Scenario zero is the raw-data baseline with no
privacy protection.

00:06:06.851 --> 00:06:11.765
Scenario one uses a single shared autoencoder to
encode one combined dataset.

00:06:11.845 --> 00:06:15.916
Scenario two simulates two peers, each with its own
autoencoder,

00:06:15.996 --> 00:06:18.056
whose embeddings are then joined.

00:06:18.136 --> 00:06:23.893
Scenarios three and four repeat one and two but
make the autoencoder non-naive: it becomes

00:06:23.973 --> 00:06:28.951
a multitask network that also predicts the
objective variable during encoding.

00:06:29.031 --> 00:06:34.075
The clear pattern is that the non-naive variants
beat their naive counterparts.

00:06:34.155 --> 00:06:35.632
On Buzz in Social Media,

00:06:35.712 --> 00:06:41.274
the test R-squared rises from about ninety-one and
a half percent with the naive shared

00:06:41.354 --> 00:06:45.684
autoencoder to about ninety-four percent with the
non-naive version,

00:06:45.764 --> 00:06:50.159
and on MNIST accuracy climbs from eighty-eight to
ninety-one percent.

00:06:50.239 --> 00:06:54.310
Guiding the latent space with the target variable
clearly helps.

00:06:54.690 --> 00:06:58.206
A few numbers capture the impact.

00:06:58.286 --> 00:07:01.802
Across all use cases,

00:07:01.882 --> 00:07:05.398
replacing raw features with shared latent
representations dropped downstream performance

00:07:05.478 --> 00:07:08.995
by less than ten percentage points,

00:07:09.075 --> 00:07:12.591
while the reconstruction error of the autoencoder
ranged from five to eleven percent.

00:07:12.671 --> 00:07:16.187
On House Pricing,

00:07:16.267 --> 00:07:19.783
test R-squared fell only from ninety point two nine
percent at baseline to eighty-nine

00:07:19.863 --> 00:07:23.379
point three three percent with two individual
autoencoders.

00:07:23.459 --> 00:07:26.975
On Buzz in Social Media,

00:07:27.055 --> 00:07:30.572
the non-naive shared autoencoder reached
ninety-four percent R-squared against a ninety-six percent baseline.

00:07:30.652 --> 00:07:34.168
And the shared autoencoders correctly estimated
between ninety-six and ninety-eight percent

00:07:34.248 --> 00:07:37.764
of observations per feature,

00:07:37.844 --> 00:07:41.360
confirming that the embeddings retain the core
structure of the data.

00:07:41.740 --> 00:07:45.681
The takeaway is simple: instead of exchanging raw
sensitive data,

00:07:45.761 --> 00:07:51.310
collaborating organizations can share the
latent-space embeddings produced by autoencoders.

00:07:51.390 --> 00:07:56.506
This keeps the original features private yet
preserves most of the predictive power,

00:07:56.586 --> 00:08:01.764
with performance dropping by less than ten
percentage points across three benchmarks.

00:08:01.844 --> 00:08:06.775
Making the autoencoder aware of the downstream task
narrows the gap even further.

00:08:06.855 --> 00:08:12.466
The authors point to custom per-dataset
autoencoders and formal measures of privacy strength

00:08:12.546 --> 00:08:16.920
as the next steps toward deploying this in real
organizational settings.
